# Every variable this service reads, and nothing else.
#
# Rule 9 of docs/ecosystem/03 §2: a repository declares the variables it needs and the deploy
# provides exactly those. `env_file: .env` fan-out — which hands every container the whole estate's
# secrets — is banned, and this file is what makes the alternative possible.
#
# Copy to `.env` for local work. `.env` is gitignored and must stay that way.
#
# Every value below is a PLACEHOLDER. Nothing here is a credential, and `src/env.ts` refuses to
# start on a known placeholder rather than booting with one — a placeholder that boots is a
# placeholder that reaches production.

# --- required --------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# This service's own database. It reads no other, and CI fails on a second connection string.
LANTERN_DATABASE_URL=postgres://lantern:lantern@127.0.0.1:5432/lantern

# ── SERVING BOTH NETWORKS FROM ONE POD ────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Both OPTIONAL and empty here: a development stack is one network. See
# micro-deploy `docs/network-consolidation.md`.
#
# Set the second DSN and this deployment holds a handle per network, choosing per
# request from the `CF-Network` header the gateway stamps. Empty, `networkSql`
# holds one handle and REFUSES a testnet request rather than answering it out of
# mainnet rows.
LANTERN_DATABASE_URL_TESTNET=
# ── WHICH ESTATE THIS DEPLOYMENT IS ───────────────────────────────────────────
#
# REQUIRED in the estate, and the render sets it for every pod. It was once
# documented here as "never in production" and that was wrong twice over:
#
#   1. The pod keys its own database handle by it. Without it a testnet
#      deployment registers its testnet DSN under the name `mainnet` and then
#      refuses every request stamped `CF-Network: testnet`.
#
#   2. SERVICE-TO-SERVICE CALLS NEVER REACH THE GATEWAY. An outbox relay POSTing
#      to another service goes container to container; nothing stamps a header
#      on it and nothing can. Without a declared network those answer 500.
#
# The header still WINS when present, so this cannot mask a mis-stamped request
# from outside — it only answers the internal callers that never had one. Leave
# it empty only where a pod genuinely serves both estates.
CF_NETWORK_SINGLE=


# Identity's JWKS and issuer, for the operator read routes. Token verification is the only thing
# this service asks identity for — and identity being unreachable does NOT lock you out: see
# LANTERN_TOKEN below.
IDENTITY_JWKS_URL=http://127.0.0.1:4001/.well-known/jwks.json
IDENTITY_ISSUER=http://127.0.0.1:4001

# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# THE CREDENTIAL WHOSE ABSENCE IS A SUPPORTED MODE IN THE SERVICE THIS REPLACES.
#
# The frozen service defaults NIMBUS_JWKS_URL and LANTERN_TOKEN to the empty string
# (stack/infra/lantern/src/env.js:35,43), and that pair of empty strings makes authMode() return
# 'open' (src/auth.js:24-31,42) — which serves every log line in the estate to anyone who can reach
# the port, after a single boot-time warning nobody re-reads. Here LANTERN_TOKEN is REQUIRED and its
# SHAPE is asserted by @cloudsforge/secrets at boot.
#
# It is presented in an `x-lantern-token` header — the same name the frozen service already reads
# (src/auth.js:49) — and it gates /metrics, which publishes which services are failing and how fast.
# It is checked BEFORE the identity token so that identity being down does not make the service
# unreadable, which is exactly when someone is reading it.
#
# Generate one: openssl rand -base64 32
#
# NOT "at least 24 characters" — that floor is gone, and its removal is micro-org #142. It could
# never fail for `estate-only-lantern-token-000000000000`, which is 38 characters and is the value
# the compose file hardcodes on two lines today. @cloudsforge/secrets refuses a placeholder MARKER
# anywhere in the value (punctuation and case stripped first, so `estate-only`, `ESTATE_ONLY` and
# `estateonly` are one rule), a JWT, anything under 16 characters, and anything degenerate enough to
# fail a Shannon entropy floor. It does NOT dictate an alphabet: this token is transcribed out of a
# runbook by whoever is holding the incident, so a value you chose yourself is accepted — a
# placeholder you chose is not. Leaving CHANGE_ME here is refused at boot, deliberately.
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
LANTERN_TOKEN=CHANGE_ME

# --- optional, with the defaults shown ---------------------------------------------------------

PORT=4010
NODE_ENV=development
LOG_LEVEL=info
LANTERN_DATABASE_POOL_MAX=10

# Names this replica in jobs.locked_by. Defaults to the hostname, which is the container id under
# compose and the pod name under Kubernetes.
# INSTANCE_ID=

# The image tag, reported on every log line.
# CLOUDSFORGE_TAG=dev

# --- the ingest limits — the hostile-input boundary --------------------------------------------
#
# Every limit REFUSES rather than repairs: a truncated protobuf is a different protobuf, so a body
# over the size ceiling is a 413 with nothing read, not a shortened message.
# LANTERN_MAX_BODY_BYTES=4194304
# LANTERN_MAX_RECORDS=5000
# LANTERN_MAX_ATTRIBUTES=128
# LANTERN_MAX_DEPTH=8
# LANTERN_MAX_STRING_BYTES=8192

# --- retention ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# An issue outlives its events — 90 over 7 — and boot refuses the inversion. Loki holds the raw
# stream; seven days here is a triage horizon, not an archive. Rollups are small and outlive both,
# which is what a 400-day error-rate trend is computed from (Prometheus cannot downsample).
# LANTERN_EVENT_RETENTION_DAYS=7
# LANTERN_ISSUE_RETENTION_DAYS=90
# LANTERN_ROLLUP_RETENTION_DAYS=400
# LANTERN_RUM_RETENTION_DAYS=30

# --- the browser RUM sink ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `name=url` origins allowed to POST client telemetry. EMPTY means the sink is OFF — "a browser may
# post to us" is a decision somebody makes, not a thing that happens because nobody set a variable.
# Never `*`: this is the one route that answers without a credential, and a wildcard there lets any
# page on the internet write into the triage view. The sink holds NO user_id, by policy.
# LANTERN_RUM_ORIGINS=https://hub.example.com,https://studio.example.com
# LANTERN_RUM_QUOTA_PER_MINUTE=120

# --- the dev-only Docker-socket collector ------------------------------------------------------
#
# OFF, and boot REFUSES to turn it on outside NODE_ENV=development: a container holding
# /var/run/docker.sock holds root on the host and can bind-mount `/` into a privileged container.
# The primary collection path is OTLP push from the collector to POST /otlp/v1/logs; this is a
# dev fallback only.
# LANTERN_DOCKER_COLLECTOR=false
# LANTERN_DOCKER_SOCKET=/var/run/docker.sock

# --- linking a trace id to a viewer ------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Where a trace id becomes a link. Optional: a deploy without Tempo publishes no link rather than a
# broken one. `{traceId}` is substituted; without it the id is appended.
# LANTERN_TRACE_URL_TEMPLATE=https://tempo.example.com/trace/{traceId}

# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# THE ANALYTICS MODULE — WAVE M1b
#
# This process is two modules. Everything below is read by `src/analytics/env.ts`, which validates
# at import exactly as `src/env.ts` does: a merged pod refuses to boot unless BOTH configurations
# are complete, rather than serving half a telemetry plane.
#
# `ANALYTICS_TOKEN` is the one variable whose MEANING changed. It gated one thing — analytics'
# `/metrics` — and this process serves lantern's instead, gated by `LANTERN_TOKEN`, because that is
# the header Prometheus, the collector and every runbook already present. So in the merged process
# `ANALYTICS_TOKEN` opens nothing. It stays REQUIRED because the standalone analytics service is
# still deployed until cutover, and because a variable that quietly becomes optional is a variable
# nobody notices has gone missing.
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# The analytics database. SEPARATE from LANTERN_DATABASE_URL, and the merged migrator REFUSES to run
# if the two address the same host, port and database: both modules own a table called `events` and
# a table called `jobs`, and both record their migrations in one table called `schema_migrations`.
# Pointed at one database, the second module's tables are silently never created and the migrator
# still exits 0.
ANALYTICS_DATABASE_URL=postgres://analytics:analytics@127.0.0.1:5432/analytics

# The analytics TESTNET database, when this deployment serves both networks. Empty means
# single-network, and the module then REFUSES a testnet request rather than answering it out of
# mainnet rows.
ANALYTICS_DATABASE_URL_TESTNET=

# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# THE PEPPER. THE MOST CONSEQUENTIAL VARIABLE IN THIS ESTATE.
#
# Every other secret answers "can an attacker act as us". This one answers "was the
# pseudonymisation ever real". With the pepper and a candidate user id, an attacker computes a
# lookup key and learns whether that person is in the event store — and, while their salt still
# exists, recovers their entire behavioural history for the four hundred days it is retained.
#
# WHERE IT LIVES: this process's memory, and the deploy's secret store. Nowhere else. It is never
# written to this service's database (there is no pepper column and there will not be one), never
# logged, never returned by a route, and never baked into an image layer.
# 13-operational-model.md:597 requires it to be absent from any backup that also contains the
# identity database — keeping it out of this database is the strongest form of that available to a
# service that owns exactly one.
#
# ROTATING IT IS NOT A DEPLOY, IT IS AN AMNESIA. Every existing lookup key becomes unreachable, so
# every returning person is minted a NEW pseudonym and their history splits in two. There is no
# re-keying path, because re-keying would require the raw subjects — which this service does not
# have, by design. Rotate only in response to disclosure, and expect every longitudinal metric to
# restart from that day.
#
# Generate one: openssl rand -base64 48 | tr -dc 'A-Za-z0-9' | cut -c1-48
#
# NOT "at least 32 characters" — that floor is gone, and its removal is micro-org #142/#189. It
# could never fail for the 40-character hyphenated `estate-only-...` pepper this estate is running
# today. @cloudsforge/secrets asserts the SHAPE of a GENERATED key instead: the base64 or hex
# alphabet (no hyphens, which is what catches every placeholder anybody here has written), 32
# decoded BYTES rather than 32 keystrokes, a measured Shannon entropy floor, and no placeholder
# marker anywhere in the value once punctuation and case are stripped. Leaving CHANGE_ME here is
# refused at boot, deliberately.
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
ANALYTICS_PSEUDONYM_KEY=CHANGE_ME

# The credential Prometheus presents in `x-analytics-token` to reach /metrics. A static token
# rather than a JWT, because Prometheus cannot mint one and identity being down is exactly when
# somebody wants this page. It is worth gating: /metrics publishes which producers are being
# refused and at what rate, which is a map of where the estate's privacy discipline is weakest.
# Generate one: openssl rand -base64 32
#
# NOT "at least 24 characters". The compose default is `estate-placeholder-token-0000000000000000`,
# 41 characters, which cleared that floor on both estates — micro-org #142. @cloudsforge/secrets
# refuses a placeholder marker anywhere in the value, a JWT, anything under 16 characters, and
# anything degenerate enough to fail an entropy floor. It does NOT dictate an alphabet: this is a
# static value an operator types, so one you chose yourself is accepted and one you copied is not.
ANALYTICS_TOKEN=CHANGE_ME

# Delivery-signature secrets for the event-bus inbox, NEWEST FIRST, comma-separated.
#
# A list rather than one value so that a rotation is a WINDOW rather than an instant: publish a new
# secret, accept both for a while, then drop the old one. One value here would make every rotation
# an estate-wide synchronised deploy. EVERY entry faces the full rule, including the one being
# rotated OUT — the outgoing key is the one an attacker already holds if it leaked, and "just for
# the drain" is how a placeholder survives the rotation meant to remove it. Two identical entries
# are refused as well: that is a rotation that did not rotate. These are GENERATED keys, so the
# strict rule applies — `openssl rand -base64 48` per entry, 32 decoded bytes minimum.
#
# This is not the same thing as the service token. The token says WHO is calling; the signature
# says the BODY was not altered between the producer's outbox and this handler. Neither implies the
# other, and both are required.
ANALYTICS_DELIVERY_SECRETS=CHANGE_ME,CHANGE_ME_PREVIOUS

# --- analytics, optional, with the defaults shown --------------------------------------------------
#
# PORT is NOT among them: one process, one listener, and it is the PORT above. `src/analytics/env.ts`
# still declares a default of its own (4023) because the standalone service still reads it; in this
# process that value is built and never used.
# ANALYTICS_DATABASE_POOL_MAX=10

# --- the minimum cohort --------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# No count derived from fewer distinct subjects than this is returned — not rounded, not as a
# range, not "fewer than five". An aggregate over few people is a disclosure, and a cohort of one
# is that person.
#
# THIS IS A FLOOR, NOT A DEFAULT. The deploy may RAISE it. `env.ts` REFUSES to start on a value
# below 5 rather than clamping it, because a suppression threshold that can be set to 1 is not a
# suppression threshold, it is a suggestion — and this is the one number that stands between a
# person and being the only member of a published cohort. See the README for why five.
# ANALYTICS_MIN_COHORT=5

# --- retention ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Enforced by a leased job that runs hourly, not by a policy in a document. `jobs.test.ts` plants a
# row past the horizon and asserts it is deleted — and asserts a row inside the horizon survives,
# because a sweep that deletes everything also passes a test that only checks the first half.
#
# Rollups outlive the events they were computed from, and boot REFUSES the inversion: a rollup that
# expired first would throw away the cheap summary while keeping the expensive raw rows.
# ANALYTICS_EVENT_RETENTION_DAYS=400
# ANALYTICS_ROLLUP_RETENTION_DAYS=1200
# ANALYTICS_INBOX_RETENTION_DAYS=30
# ANALYTICS_IDEMPOTENCY_TTL_DAYS=30

# How many weeks wide the retention heatmap is. Metric 18 says twelve.
# ANALYTICS_COHORT_WEEKS=12

# --- not read by this service ------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT and the rest of OTEL_* are read by the OpenTelemetry SDK loaded ahead
# of the process, not by src/env.ts. They belong in the deploy, not here.
#
# LANTERN_TEST_DATABASE_URL and ANALYTICS_TEST_DATABASE_URL are read only by the test suite, and
# only when the name contains "test" — the database-backed tests truncate. CI exports BOTH, into two
# SEPARATE databases, and fails the build if either half's suites skip. `pnpm test` needs both: with
# only the lantern DSN set, ten analytics suites skip and the run reports 367 pass / 0 fail instead
# of 615.
